(a) Exclusive of the license required herein, each alcoholic beverage caterer wishing to sell or distribute distilled spirits, beer, malt beverages, or wine at an authorized catered event, function, or special event within the city must apply for an event permit. The application for the event shall include the following:
(1) The name of the caterer and the alcoholic beverage caterer's license number;
(2) The date, location, time, duration, and name of the event;
(3) The sponsoring organizations, nature of the event, general purpose thereof, and whether the proceeds are for a for-profit or nonprofit organization;
(4) An application for a certificate of special event insurance, covering BOTH the event itself and all persons or parties responsible for the dispensing of alcoholic beverages at the event;
(5) Proof that the applicant has also applied for a special event permit with the Georgia Department of Revenue;
(6) The quantity of alcoholic beverages to be transported from the licensee's primary location to the location of the event;
(7) The persons who are responsible for the special event, including their names, addresses, relevant contact information, and Social Security number or other identifying information;
(8) Such other information as might be required by this chapter and/or the building inspector, so as to be able to fully review all aspects of the special event application.
(b) All caterers licensed pursuant to this division shall pay excise taxes at the rates set forth in this article.
(1) Caterers licensed by the city shall maintain a record of all alcoholic beverages transported for each event by event, and shall submit their reports and remit taxes due with their monthly reports to the city.
(2) Caterers licensed by any other jurisdiction shall maintain a record of all alcoholic beverages transported into the city for any event and submit a report of such beverages to the city within three days after the conclusion of the event. Failure to submit the reports and taxes required by this section may be grounds for denial of future permits to any caterer for similar events.
(c) Each application shall be accompanied with a fee as approved by the City of Cedartown Commission in the schedule of fees and charges, which shall defray the cost incurred in background investigations, review of the permit, and related matters by the building inspector, and, potentially, by the city manager. The application fee is nonrefundable. In the event a permit is granted, an additional fee as approved by the City of Cedartown Commission in the schedule of fees and charges shall be paid. With each application, a certified check for the application fee shall accompany the application. No permit shall be granted until the fee as approved by the City of Cedartown Commission in the schedule of fees and charges is paid.
(d) The original permit shall be kept in the vehicle at all times while transporting the alcoholic beverages to the authorized catered event or function or special event. It also shall be maintained at the site of the catered event and available for inspection both at and during such event or function or special event.
(e) It is unlawful for a licensed alcoholic beverage caterer to distribute or sell alcoholic beverages off the premises of the special event or in any manner other than as authorized by the event permit. All sales or service of alcoholic beverages at the event shall take place within the primary structure of the building, within certain defined areas of the special event location, and within the basic confines of the location (excluding areas of service of alcoholic beverages), as described in the application of the caterer. No alcoholic beverages may be sold or served outside of the confines of the general location of the special event; nor, will any patron of the special event be allowed to leave the special event location with alcoholic beverages.
(f) The event permit will be valid only for the length of the authorized catered event or function or special event or as approved by the city, not to exceed three days. After expiration of the event, the alcoholic beverage caterer must apply for a new event permit in the same manner as the original permit.
(g) The alcoholic beverage caterer shall provide one person per 100 people at the special event to serve as security for said event.
(h) Within not less than five days prior to the special event the caterer shall provide the building inspector with an insurance certificate, evidencing coverage for the special event and all persons serving alcoholic beverages at the special event location. This insurance certificate shall name the city as an additional named insured, together with any department of the city that may be the recipient of any proceeds of that special event, or otherwise may be involved in assisting with the special event in any manner. The insurance coverage shall be a minimum of $1,000,000.00 per occurrence, and $100,000.00 in property damage coverage, evidenced on the face of the certificate.
(i) No employee, agent, elected official, servant, or other party affiliated in any manner with the city shall receive any percentage of the proceeds, individually (and not as a part of their official function with the city), for their involvement in the event, except as to their general rate of pay or other similar remuneration to said individual through the city. This limitation shall not apply to events and/or performances held at the Cedartown Civic Auditorium, at which the Cedartown Auditorium Commission serves as the alcoholic beverage caterer.
(Ord. No. 17,2010, § 2(5), 11-8-10; Ord. No. 1-2012, § 2, 1-9-12; Ord. No. 7-2019, § 2(Exh. A), 12-9-19)

